Lea Verou grew up on the Greek island of Lesbos. She began freelancing as a web developer in 2005 while still at university, studying computer science at the Athens University of Economics and Business. In 2008 she co-founded a Greek web company, which was sold in 2013.

## Open-source tools

Verou has started more than thirty open-source projects. The best known is Prism, a syntax highlighter that colors code on websites. Many other projects help designers work with CSS and color, including Color.js.

## Shaping web standards

- **2012:** Joined the W3C as a developer advocate.
- **2015:** Published *CSS Secrets*, a book of clever solutions to everyday design problems.
- **Later:** Earned a master's degree at MIT and researched how to make building web apps easier for non-programmers, with a project called Mavo.
- **Later:** Was elected to the W3C Technical Architecture Group (TAG), which guides the design of the web as a whole, and became an invited expert in the CSS Working Group.

She has spoken at more than a hundred conferences. Her talks are known for live coding with a presentation tool she built herself, so audiences can watch the CSS change on screen as she types.

